\begin{document}

\title{\textbf{The $T$ Gate is Not a Braid}\\[0.3em]
\large A cyclotomic obstruction in the Fibonacci anyon model}

\author{C.\ Lando\ Mills%
\thanks{The author thanks Harry T.\ Larson; see Acknowledgements and \cite{Larson1961}.}}

\date{6 August 2026}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Fibonacci braid group image is dense in $PSU(2)$, so every gate is approximable and
none is obviously exact. We settle which single-qubit phase gates are exact.

$Z$ is: it is $\sigma_1^{5}$. $S$ and $T$ are not, at any word length, up to any global
phase, and no encoding basis recovers them, the obstruction being on the spectrum rather
than on the entries.

The reason is one sign. Braid matrices lie in $K = \mathbb{Q}(\zeta_5,\sqrt\varphi)$,
whose real subfield is $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt\varphi)$; the norm of $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt5)$
carries a square to the square of a rational, and $N(\varphi) = -1$. Two invariants are
needed, not one: $\operatorname{tr}^2/\det$ excludes $T$ and is blind to $S$, and the
determinant lattice, every generator contributing a primitive tenth root of unity,
excludes $S$.

This raises $8 \nmid 10$ from a statement about the generators to one about every
word. Compilation of $S$ and $T$
here is arithmetic necessity, not engineering accommodation. The argument is formalized
in Lean 4 with no sorries.
\end{abstract}

