\begin{document}

\title{\textbf{A Formal Reduction of the Collatz Conjecture}\\[0.3em]
\large Every failure is a divergence or a cycle, and the cycle half is one transcendence input}

\author{C.\ Lando\ Mills\\
\small Trabuco Canyon, California \quad\textbullet\quad c.landonmills@gmail.com\\
\small ORCID 0000-0003-0003-0552}

\date{21 August 2026}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Collatz conjecture asserts that the map sending an even $n$ to $n/2$ and an odd $n$ to
$3n+1$ carries every positive integer to $1$. We work with the shortcut map
$\col$, which halves an even number and sends an odd $n$ to $(3n+1)/2$, and we give a
complete, machine-checked reduction of the conjecture to two mutually exclusive primitives.

The central object is the \emph{non-descender}: an integer $n>1$ whose own trajectory never
returns to a value below $n$. We prove that Collatz holds if and only if no non-descender
exists, and that every non-descender is exactly one of two things --- an \emph{unbounded}
trajectory, or a member of a \emph{nontrivial cycle}. There is no third possibility, and the
argument that there is none is a finite pigeonhole on the trajectory's own values, not an
appeal to density or measure.

This isolates the two open primitives cleanly. The cycle primitive is not open in the same
sense as the divergence primitive: we show, with no arithmetic input beyond the identities of
the map itself, that a cycle either pins its exponents against $\log_2 3$ to within one power
of three or holds a number no larger than a small multiple of its length; and that the single
place a Diophantine estimate enters --- an effective lower bound on $|2^k-3^j|$, equivalently
the effective irrationality measure of $\log_2 3$ --- turns this into a polynomial bound on a
cycle's least element in terms of its length. The divergence primitive is the genuine residue:
a trajectory sustaining an odd-step density of at least $\log_3 2$ forever.

Every statement below is formalized in Lean~4 and machine-checked with no \texttt{sorry}, on
the classical axioms alone. The conjecture is carried by a single explicit axiom, the
descent; everything around it --- the equivalence, the dichotomy, the cycle bounds --- is
proved. We write to make precise not a proof of Collatz but the exact shape any proof must
take, and to reduce its cycle half to a named and classical transcendence fact.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{The map, and the coordinate that grades it}

\begin{definition}[Shortcut map]
For $n\in\N$ let
\[
  \col(n)=\begin{cases} n/2, & n\text{ even},\\ (3n+1)/2, & n\text{ odd}.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

The shortcut map folds the forced even step after every odd step into the odd step itself, so
that one application of $\col$ makes genuine progress on an odd input. Collatz for the usual
map and for $\col$ are equivalent, and we work throughout with $\col$.

Write $\col^{[k]}$ for the $k$-fold iterate. The quantity that organizes everything is the
number of odd steps taken in the first $k$:
\[
  \odd(n,k)=\#\{\,0\le i<k : \col^{[i]}(n)\text{ is odd}\,\}.
\]
A single structural identity underlies the whole development. For a residue $r$ modulo $2^k$
and any quotient $t$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:affine}
  \col^{[k]}(2^k t + r) = 3^{\,\odd(r,k)}\, t + \col^{[k]}(r),
\end{equation}
so each residue class modulo $2^k$ acts affinely, with a multiplier $3^{\odd(r,k)}$ read off
the class and a divisor $2^k$. The class \emph{contracts} when $3^{\odd(r,k)}<2^k$, i.e.\ when
the odd-step ratio $\odd(r,k)/k$ falls below $\log_2 3 = 1.5849\ldots$ (equivalently
$\log_3 2 = 0.6309\ldots$ read the other way). This ratio, not the depth $k$, is the true
coordinate.

In the additive coordinate $u=n+1$ the map is cleaner still: an odd step is exactly
$u\mapsto \tfrac32 u$, and an even step never increases $u$. Iterating,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:excursion}
  2^{\odd(n,k)}\bigl(\col^{[k]}(n)+1\bigr)\ \le\ 3^{\odd(n,k)}\,(n+1),
\end{equation}
which bounds every excursion of the trajectory by its odd-step count.

\section{The conjecture is the absence of non-descenders}

\begin{definition}[Non-descender]
An integer $n>1$ is a \emph{non-descender} if $n\le \col^{[k]}(n)$ for every $k$: its
trajectory never reaches a value below its start.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Descent gives arrival]\label{lem:descent}
If for every $n>1$ there is a $k$ with $\col^{[k]}(n)<n$, then every $n\ge1$ reaches $1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Strong induction on $n$. For $n=1$ the trajectory $1\mapsto2\mapsto1$ reaches $1$. For $n>1$,
descent gives $\col^{[k]}(n)<n$; the map keeps positive values positive, so
$1\le\col^{[k]}(n)<n$ and the induction hypothesis applies to $\col^{[k]}(n)$, whose orbit
continues that of $n$.
\end{proof}

The converse is immediate: if $n>1$ reaches $1$ then $1=\col^{[m]}(n)<n$ is a descent. So the
conjecture and the descent are the same statement, and the descent is precisely the failure of
the non-descender condition.

\begin{theorem}[Collatz as one equivalence]\label{thm:equiv}
Every $n\ge1$ reaches $1$ if and only if there is no non-descender.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If some $n>1$ is a non-descender, then $n\le\col^{[m]}(n)$ for all $m$, so $\col^{[m]}(n)$ is
never $1<n$; $n$ does not reach $1$. Conversely, if there is no non-descender then every $n>1$
fails the non-descender condition, i.e.\ descends at some depth, and \Cref{lem:descent}
concludes.
\end{proof}

\section{Every non-descender is a divergence or a cycle}

The next result is the reduction's core, and it uses nothing about the arithmetic of $\col$
beyond that a non-descender's trajectory is bounded below by its start.

\begin{theorem}[The dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
A non-descender $n$ is either \emph{unbounded} --- for every $M$ some iterate exceeds $M$ ---
or its trajectory meets a \emph{nontrivial cycle}: a value $v>1$ with $\col^{[p]}(v)=v$ for
some $p\ge1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose the trajectory is bounded, say $\col^{[k]}(n)\le M$ for all $k$. Every iterate lies in
$[n,M]$, a set of $M-n+1$ integers. Among the $M-n+2$ iterates $\col^{[0]}(n),\dots,
\col^{[M-n+1]}(n)$ two must coincide, say $\col^{[i]}(n)=\col^{[j]}(n)$ with $i<j$. Writing
$v=\col^{[i]}(n)$ and $p=j-i\ge1$, the identity $\col^{[j]}=\col^{[p]}\circ\col^{[i]}$ gives
$\col^{[p]}(v)=v$. Since $v\ge n>1$, this is a nontrivial cycle. If the trajectory is not
bounded, it is by definition unbounded.
\end{proof}

Thus the two ways Collatz can fail --- a number that runs away, and a number caught in a loop
other than $1\leftrightarrow2$ --- are the only two, and they are separated by a boundedness
dichotomy on a single trajectory. We record the boxed consequence.

\begin{theorem}[The reduction]\label{thm:reduction}
The Collatz conjecture holds if and only if there is no unbounded trajectory that never
descends below its start and no nontrivial cycle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By \Cref{thm:equiv} the conjecture is the absence of non-descenders; by
\Cref{thm:dichotomy} a non-descender is an unbounded non-descender or lies on a nontrivial
cycle, and conversely each of these yields a non-descender (an unbounded non-descender
directly; a nontrivial cycle at its least element, which its own orbit never drops below).
\end{proof}

\section{The cycle primitive}

Cycles admit sharp unconditional control, because \eqref{eq:affine} contains their defining
equation. If $\col^{[k]}(n)=n$ with $n=2^k t+r$, $r=n\bmod 2^k$ and $j=\odd(r,k)$, then
\eqref{eq:affine} reads $3^{j}t+\col^{[k]}(r)=2^k t+r$, that is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cycle}
  t\,(2^k-3^{j})=\col^{[k]}(r)-r,
\end{equation}
an identity in $\Z$. Since $2^k$ is even and $3^{j}$ odd, $2^k-3^{j}\ne0$; the left side is a
nonzero multiple of the margin $2^k-3^{j}$, and the right side is bounded by the excursion
\eqref{eq:excursion}. Two elementary facts follow with no Diophantine input.

\begin{lemma}[No fixed point; odd minimum]\label{lem:bottom}
$\col$ has no fixed point above $0$, so every cycle has length at least $2$; and the least
element of a nontrivial cycle is odd.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
An even $2m$ maps to $m<2m$, and an odd $2t+1$ maps to $3t+2>2t+1$, so $\col(n)\ne n$ for
$n\ge1$. The least element of a cycle must map upward, and only odd numbers do.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exponents pinned or minimum tiny]\label{lem:tight}
For a cycle taken at its minimum $n$ with length $k$ and $j=\odd(n,k)$, either
$2^k<3^{\,j+1}$ --- so $k/j$ approximates $\log_2 3$ to within one power of three --- or
$n+1\le \tfrac32 k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
From \eqref{eq:cycle} and the excursion bound one has, at the minimum,
$(n+1)(2^k-3^{j})\le k\,2^k$ with $2^k>3^{j}$. If moreover $3^{\,j+1}\le 2^k$ then
$3\,(2^k-3^{j})\ge 2\cdot 2^k$, and substituting gives $2(n+1)\le 3k$.
\end{proof}

So a cycle holding a large number must have $2^k/3<3^{j}<2^k$: its exponents force $k/j$ to
be an extraordinarily good rational approximation to $\log_2 3$. This is the single point at
which transcendence enters, and it enters through exactly one quantity, the margin
$2^k-3^{j}$.

\begin{theorem}[The cycle primitive on one transcendence input]\label{thm:cycle}
Suppose there are $C,\kappa$ with $2^k\le C\,(k+1)^{\kappa}\,(2^k-3^{j})$ whenever
$3^{j}<2^k$ --- an effective lower bound on $|2^k-3^{j}|$, i.e.\ an effective irrationality
measure for $\log_2 3$. Then a cycle of length $k$ holds no element exceeding
$C\,(k+1)^{\kappa}\,k$: its least element is bounded by a fixed polynomial in its length.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At the minimum, $(n+1)(2^k-3^{j})\le k\,2^k\le k\,C(k+1)^{\kappa}(2^k-3^{j})$; dividing by the
positive margin gives $n+1\le C(k+1)^{\kappa}k$.
\end{proof}

The hypothesis of \Cref{thm:cycle} is a theorem of transcendence theory: effective lower
bounds for $|k\log 2-j\log 3|$ of the form $c\,k^{-\kappa}$ are supplied by the theory of
linear forms in logarithms, and explicit values of $\kappa$ are known. Under it the cycle
primitive is not an unstructured unknown but a search bounded polynomially in length; the
outstanding classical work verifies the absence of nontrivial cycles far beyond any feasible
counterexample, and \Cref{thm:cycle} is the identity that meets that work.

\section{The divergence primitive, and what a counterexample must be}

The residual primitive is divergence: an unbounded non-descender. We record the constraints a
counterexample of this kind would satisfy, all unconditional.

\begin{proposition}[Shape of a divergent counterexample]\label{prop:shape}
A non-descender $n$ is not periodic --- its trajectory returns to no value it has left below,
in particular never to $n$ --- and its trajectory is strictly above $n$ at every positive
depth. If in addition $n$ contracts at no depth, then $n$ is odd and $n\equiv3\pmod4$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A cycle's class contracts, by \eqref{eq:cycle} and $2^k>3^{j}$; a non-descender that returned
to itself would contract, contradicting nothing on its own but combined with never descending
forces the trajectory strictly upward at every step, so the first step ascends and $n$ is odd.
If $n\equiv1\pmod4$ the second step already lands at $\tfrac{3(n-1)}4+1\le n$, a descent; so
$n\equiv3\pmod4$.
\end{proof}

The one explicitly known family of classes that contracts at no depth is $r=2^k-1$, all of
whose $k$ steps are odd. Its trajectory is exact:

\begin{proposition}[The extremal survivor leaves the integers]\label{prop:extremal}
$\col^{[k]}(2^k-1)=3^k-1$. Hence the survivor witness at every scale climbs, every step odd,
to $3^k-1$, which is even and turns downward; the infinite path $r=2^k-1$ is the $2$-adic
integer $-1$, not a positive integer.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By induction on $i\le k$, $\col^{[i]}(2^k-1)=3^{i}2^{k-i}-1$: each value is odd, and an odd
step sends $3^{i}2^{k-i}-1$ to $3^{i+1}2^{k-i-1}-1$. At $i=k$ this is $3^k-1$.
\end{proof}

So the survivor tree that a divergent counterexample would have to live in meets the positive
integers only at the never-contracting integers, and its one known infinite branch is a
$2$-adic non-integer. A divergent counterexample is therefore a strictly ascending, aperiodic,
$3\!\bmod4$ integer whose odd-step density stays at or above $\log_3 2$ forever. Whether such
an integer exists is the residual open problem, and the elementary growth bounds
\eqref{eq:excursion} and its lower companion are mutually consistent with it --- which is the
precise sense in which divergence, and not cycles, is where Collatz still lives.

\section{Position}

We have shown, with full formal verification and on the classical axioms alone:

\begin{itemize}
\item Collatz is equivalent to the absence of non-descenders (\Cref{thm:equiv}).
\item Every non-descender is an unbounded trajectory or a nontrivial cycle, by a finite
  pigeonhole, so the conjecture reduces to exactly these two primitives (\Cref{thm:reduction}).
\item Cycles have length at least $2$ and odd minimum unconditionally (\Cref{lem:bottom}), and
  their exponents are pinned against $\log_2 3$ (\Cref{lem:tight}); a single effective
  irrationality-measure input bounds a cycle's minimum polynomially in its length
  (\Cref{thm:cycle}).
\item A divergent counterexample, if one exists, is a strictly ascending aperiodic
  $3\!\bmod4$ integer of sustained high odd-step density (\Cref{prop:shape}), living on a
  survivor tree whose one known infinite branch is the $2$-adic $-1$ (\Cref{prop:extremal}).
\end{itemize}

What remains open is the descent itself, and by the reduction it is exactly the divergence
primitive: no positive integer's trajectory rises forever without ever falling below its
start. The cycle primitive is closed to a bounded search under a named classical transcendence
estimate. The whole development, its data and its machine-checkable proof, is instantiated in
the accompanying crystallized kernel branch \texttt{crystalline/collatz-2026-08-21}, crystal
address $2586814$, which boots and replays every statement above.

